title: a1.1 Mindful Walk
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Five activities. Three walks, by company: alone (SA-01), with a fellow resident (SA-02), with a relative on the phone (SA-03). Two occur in all three: losing and finding the thread of a conversation (SA-04), and answering a painful question (SA-05). Fresh air and movement stimulate memory and steady emotions; company gives a reason to go. Breakdowns: nobody to talk to, a partner who does not fit, a voice that cannot see, slips that cost face, answers that are untrue or come from the wrong person.

= Situated Activity - A walk on your own [SA-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-01
|Name|A walk on your own
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only.

* Forbes et al. (2015), Cochrane review: exercise may improve daily functioning in dementia
* Bratman et al. (2015): a walk in nature lowered rumination (healthy adults)
* Detweiler et al. (2008): an enclosed garden on a dementia unit; fewer agitated behaviours (one unit, before-after)
* Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes is common
)))
|Goal|Go out when you like: fresh air, movement, a change of scene. Back safely.
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - walks alone
* ST-04: Care worker - opens the door; notices who is out
* ST-06: Physiotherapist - assesses walking unaccompanied
)))
|Trigger|Restless; fine weather; garden door open; an old habit (the dog's walk)
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Decide to go
1. Find the door; coat, shoes, rollator
1. Walk the loop; look, listen
1. Rest on a bench
1. Notice when to turn back: tired, cold
1. Find the door; let staff know
)))
|Context|Only residents assessed fit (ST-06). Enclosed garden loop, one coded gate. Nobody to talk to; staff glance out of the window. Some residents talk to passers-by, or to themselves.
|Frequency and duration|Varies widely; 5-30 min; mostly in fine weather
|Tools and artifacts|Garden door, loop path, benches, rollator, a window onto the garden
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Loop that leads back to the door
* Door open in fine weather; staff keep an eye out
* Accompanied walk when walking alone is not allowed
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* Forgets where or why; back inside after a few minutes
* Nobody to talk to: lonely; stops going out
* Asks a passing worker a painful question (SA-05)
* Fall, unseen
* Tired, far from the door
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01 (supported by ST-04, ST-06); extended-by → SA-02, SA-03; includes → SA-05; illustrated-by → PS-03.

= Situated Activity - A walk with a fellow resident [SA-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-02
|Name|A walk with a fellow resident
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only.

* de Medeiros et al. (2012): residents with dementia form and keep friendships
* Lundin-Olsson et al. (1997): older people who stop walking when they talk fall more often
)))
|Goal|Company as the reason to go out; a conversation both enjoy
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - walks, talks
* ST-02: Fellow resident - walks, talks
* ST-05: Activity coordinator - matches pairs
* ST-04: Care worker - starts the walk; keeps an eye out
)))
|Trigger|Staff see two residents who get along; one invites the other; a pair's habit
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Match: pace, mood, stage of dementia, who gets along with whom
1. Ask both
1. Introduce, or re-introduce
1. Walk side by side, at one pace
1. Talk: what they see, what they share
1. Rest on a bench
1. One wants to go back: both turn
)))
|Context|The match makes or breaks the walk. Staff know some shared pasts, and who dislikes whom, informally. Names are forgotten. Both may have dementia: neither notices the other's slips (SA-04). The more able partner can slip into minding the other. Talking slows walking and raises fall risk.
|Frequency and duration|Occasional; 15-30 min
|Tools and artifacts|Staff knowledge, care record, life-story book, garden, benches
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Pair residents who sit together at meals, or share a past
* Re-introduce them at each walk
* Same pair, same time, same route
* Care worker walks along
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* No partner; mismatch in pace or mood
* Two residents who dislike each other, paired by someone who did not know
* Agrees to please staff or the partner: "I suppose so"
* Talk dries up; a slip goes unnoticed
* Stumbles while talking; tiredness missed while the talk is good
* One wants to go back; the other's walk ends too
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01, ST-02 (supported by ST-04, ST-05); extends → SA-01; includes → SA-04; illustrated-by → PS-01.

= Situated Activity - A walk "on the phone" with a relative [SA-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-03
|Name|A walk "on the phone" with a relative
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only. No study found of residents walking while on the phone.

* Zamir et al. (2018): video calls in care homes; staff time, hearing and equipment stood in the way
* Montero-Odasso et al. (2012): with cognitive impairment, walking slows and grows unsteady under a second task
)))
|Goal|Stay in touch between visits; company from someone who knows your life
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - walks, talks
* ST-03: Relative - calls, talks
* ST-04: Care worker - dials; keeps an eye out
)))
|Trigger|Agreed call time; the relative calls; the resident asks for them
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Agree a time
1. Phone charged; hearing aid in
1. Answer, or staff dial
1. Recognise the voice
1. Walk and talk at once
1. Say what is around; hear the news
1. End; agree the next call
)))
|Context|Relatives often live far away; visits weekly or less. Voice only: no face, no gesture; hearing loss, wind. A rollator needs both hands: speaker on, others hear. The relative cannot see a stumble, tiredness or a wrong turn. The relative knows the life story, yet "What did you do today?" fails.
|Frequency and duration|Calls weekly or more, 5-20 min; on a walk, rare
|Tools and artifacts|Mobile or ward phone, speaker, hearing aid, call times on the ward board
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Staff dial, then pass the phone
* Fixed call time
* Relative says who they are at once; asks about old times
* Call during an accompanied walk
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* Missed call; voice not recognised; cannot hear
* Story repeated; relative says "You told me" (SA-04)
* "Come and get me": no answer that helps
* Stumbles while talking; the relative cannot see or help
* Calls grow rarer
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01, ST-03 (supported by ST-04); extends → SA-01; includes → SA-04; illustrated-by → PS-02.

= Situated Activity - Losing and finding the thread [SA-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-04
|Name|Losing and finding the thread
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only.

* Orange et al. (1996): in Alzheimer's disease, trouble in conversation grows and repair succeeds less often
* Schegloff et al. (1977): speakers prefer to fix their own slips
* Bora et al. (2015), meta-analysis: theory of mind is impaired in Alzheimer's disease
* Clare (2003): people aware of decline protect themselves, or face it
)))
|Goal|Keep talking after a slip, without losing face
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - slips, tries again
* ST-02 or ST-03: Partner or relative - waits, helps
* ST-04: Care worker - helps in passing
)))
|Trigger|A slip: thread lost mid-story; a word that will not come; a story told again; a belief from another time ("My mother is waiting"); someone not recognised
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Notice the slip
1. Give time
1. Speaker tries again
1. Partner helps: the last topic, a new one, the surroundings
1. Talk resumes, changes topic, or stops
)))
|Context|Slips grow more frequent, and repair less successful, as dementia progresses. A partner with dementia often misses the slip. Many residents know they slipped and feel it. Relatives repair best, but tire of repetition. Walking gives pauses and things to look at.
|Frequency and duration|Many times per conversation; more when tired, later in the day
|Tools and artifacts|Shared knowledge, life-story book, the surroundings
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Gentle redirection: to the present, to the feeling, to a new topic
* Finish the sentence for them
* "Do you remember...?"
* "You told me that already"
* Play along with an untrue belief
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* Nobody notices: silence; the walk ends
* Helped too fast: the speaker loses the turn
* Corrected or quizzed: embarrassed ("My head's no good any more")
* Played along: trust lost when found out
* After repeated slips: avoids talk and company
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01 with ST-02 or ST-03; occurs-in → SA-01 to SA-03; illustrated-by → PS-01, PS-02.

= Situated Activity - Answering a painful question [SA-05] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-05
|Name|Answering a painful question
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only.

* Mental Health Foundation (2016): truth-telling and lying are both common in dementia care; practice is inconsistent
* James et al. (2006): staff lie to residents, mostly to spare distress
)))
|Goal|A truthful answer, from someone the resident trusts, without new harm
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - asks
* ST-04: Care worker - answers, stays
* ST-03: Relative - agrees what is said; sometimes answers
* ST-06: Physician - tells medical news
)))
|Trigger|"Where is my husband?"; "When can I go home?"; "What is wrong with me?"; a death or a diagnosis
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Recognise the question as sensitive
1. Check what was agreed, and with whom
1. Find the person who should answer
1. Answer: truthful, short, kind
1. Stay; comfort
1. Note it for the next shift
)))
|Context|Asked of whoever is near: a new worker, a fellow resident, a voice on the phone. Agreements are in care plans or in staff heads, not always passed on. A death may be news each time it is told. Residents know things about each other, true or not.
|Frequency and duration|Daily on a ward; some residents several times a day
|Tools and artifacts|Care plan, handover notes, agreements with family
|Existing workarounds|(((
* An agreed answer in the care plan
* Call the key worker
* Change the subject
* A kind lie ("He's at work")
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* Told bluntly by someone who did not know: grief as if new
* A kind lie found out: trust lost
* Different answers from different staff
* A fellow resident or relative says too much
* No answer: the question returns
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-04 with ST-01 (supported by ST-03, ST-06); occurs-in → SA-01 to SA-03; illustrated-by → PS-03.

= The three walks compared =

|=Aspect|=SA-01 Alone|=SA-02 Fellow resident|=SA-03 Relative on the phone
|Company|None|Present; often has dementia too|Remote; knows the life story
|Notices a slip|Nobody|The partner, often not|The relative, by ear
|Notices a stumble|Whoever looks out|The partner, if able|Nobody: voice only
|Hears a painful question|Whoever passes|The partner, unprepared|The relative, if agreed

